## Lawn: the pricing difference is significant

Bates' base pricing is:

| Property | Bates |
|---|---:|
| Main Campus | $19,075.10 |
| Cemeteries | $27,684.96 |
| **Base combined** | **$46,760.06** |

When I add Bates' quoted fertilization, second cemetery fall cleanup, campus weed control, additional pruning and fertilization to make the comparison more similar to Jastrem, Bates comes to approximately **$57,880.66**.

Jastrem's service subtotal is **$76,581.50**, with a displayed total after tax of **$77,650.49**.

That puts Jastrem approximately **$18,701, or 32.3%, above the expanded Bates comparison**.

But that comparison is still not fair enough to award the contract. Bates gets part of its savings by proposing substantial cemetery acreage become meadow/natural grass and only receive field mowing twice annually. Jastrem is currently pricing much more regular cemetery mowing.

That is why I recommend **not using either current headline price for final scoring**.

### Bates lawn

Bates has several strengths. Its campus proposal is detailed, provides **26 mowing visits**, spring/fall cleanup, pruning, mulch and four free account-manager inspections. It also recognizes the school/playground environment and important Parish holidays.

The major problem is the cemetery proposal. Bates explicitly says the goal is to reduce regular mowing and move the **upper fields** toward field/meadow mowing twice a year. I agree with your concern: **too much of Saint Ann Cemetery becomes natural grass under the Bates proposal.**

The Parish—not Bates—should determine exactly which property becomes naturalized.

There are also contract provisions I would reject or renegotiate: Bates' standard agreement says its agreement controls in a conflict; charges **2.5% per month/30% annualized** on late invoices; provides a 25% minimum collection charge; allows fairly broad extra-work/change-order triggers; allows certain material substitution in its discretion; and contains evergreen-renewal language.

### Jastrem lawn

Jastrem's proposal is operationally cleaner because all three properties are incorporated into one program. It also uses the better turf-management concept of removing no more than one-third of the grass blade per mowing rather than simply mowing on a calendar.

However, Jastrem is currently **over-maintaining Saint Ann Cemetery relative to what you now want**, which is contributing substantially to its $39,866.40 Saint Ann Cemetery mowing price.

For the revised Jastrem proposal, I specifically recommend:

> **The Parish-designated first half of Saint Ann Cemetery shall be maintained as a natural-grass/meadow zone, except for identified roadway buffers, entrances, active burial areas, visitor/monument corridors, sight lines, and emergency-access areas.**

The words **"first half" should not appear by themselves in the contract**. It needs to be drawn on a map and signed by both parties.

Then require Jastrem to show the actual dollar reduction from the current **$39,866.40 Saint Ann Cemetery mowing charge**. They should provide separate rates for regular mowing and natural/meadow maintenance. Otherwise Saint Ann cannot determine whether the reduction in scope produces a fair reduction in price.

For fairness, **Bates must then price that exact same map**. Bates should no longer be permitted to choose a much larger natural area.

That single step will finally give you a legitimate apples-to-apples comparison.

