# Changes I recommend before either award

The largest improvement is to issue **one identical Best and Final Offer (BAFO) addendum to both companies**. It should establish the signed lawn-zone maps, identical natural-grass boundaries, school safety periods, cemetery/funeral requirements, snow SLAs, equipment commitments and the same pricing assumptions.

I also recommend making the contract hierarchy explicit:

> **Final Saint Ann Contract/Addendum → Saint Ann RFP and maps → Vendor BAFO → Vendor Proposal → Vendor standard terms.**

This prevents Bates' or Jastrem's boilerplate from quietly overriding the RFP.

For lawn care, I added requirements for a 3–3.5-inch manicured turf standard, the one-third rule, clipping removal, cemetery monument protection, electronic service reporting, natural-zone management, funeral coordination and Holy Family School restrictions of **7:40–8:15 AM and 2:40–3:15 PM**.

For snow, I added the **7:00 AM school-day readiness requirement**, mandatory **36-hour cemetery clearing**, documented refreeze monitoring, no ordinary holiday surcharge, controls on T&M, written approval for snow hauling, and protection against double billing a snowfall tier plus T&M for the same ordinary work.

I also recommend changing the procurement process so certain items are **pass/fail before scoring**: current COI, applicable pesticide credentials, three references, staffing/equipment commitment, subcontractor disclosure, signed maps, school-safety acceptance and the cemetery snow SLA.

Neither proposal currently gives Saint Ann enough of that information.

## Contract terms worth tightening

I would negotiate toward **Net 30**, no mandatory credit card on file, no automatic evergreen renewal, written Parish authorization for renewal, and a 30-day Parish convenience-termination provision.

I would also eliminate unilateral fuel/material price changes. If Saint Ann accepts escalation at all, it should be based on a defined published index/formula with an annual cap.

For a multi-year agreement, I would prefer a clearly specified second/third-year increase—such as an agreed CPI-U methodology with a maximum cap—rather than an automatic undefined increase.

Insurance limits should be finalized with the Parish insurer/counsel, but the addendum I prepared uses commercial benchmarks such as **$1M CGL occurrence/$2M aggregate, $1M commercial auto, statutory workers compensation and an agreed umbrella limit** as negotiating placeholders.

